narrative:
## Simulating Default Values

XIOM doesn't have built-in default parameter values, but you can create the same effect by writing two functions: one with a parameter, and a simpler one that calls it with a default.

### The Pattern

```xiom
fn greet_person(name: Str) -> Str {
  return "Hello, " + name + "!";
}

fn greet() -> Str {
  return greet_person("friend");
}

fn main() -> Int {
  io.println(greet());              // Hello, friend!
  io.println(greet_person("Kilo")); // Hello, Kilo!
  return 0;
}
```

`greet()` is the shortcut — it calls `greet_person` with "friend" as the default name. `greet_person("Kilo")` is the full version when you want a specific name.

### Why This Pattern?

```xiom
fn set_volume(level: Int) {
  io.println("Volume set to: ");
  io.println(level);
}

fn set_default_volume() {
  set_volume(50);  // 50 is the default
}

fn main() -> Int {
  set_default_volume();  // Volume: 50
  set_volume(75);        // Volume: 75
  return 0;
}
```

### Multiple Defaults

You can have a function with multiple parameters and provide a default version that fills in ALL the common values:

```xiom
fn create_profile(name: Str, age: Int, country: Str) {
  io.println("Profile: " + name + ", " + " years old, from " + country);
}

fn create_default_profile() {
  create_profile("Anonymous", 0, "Unknown");
}

fn main() -> Int {
  create_default_profile();
  create_profile("Kilo", 2, "XIOM Land");
  return 0;
}
```

This pattern keeps your code DRY (Don't Repeat Yourself). The logic lives in one place — the parameterized function — and the shortcut version just calls it with defaults.

